---
// "Your agent uses your vault, not your passwords" on the landing (M10, D-038). The
// session is illustrative: the commands, the --json shape and the exit codes are the real
// CLI's (reference/cli.md); hosts and addresses are the demo's.
---

<section class="flint-agent not-content" aria-labelledby="flint-agent-title">
	<p class="flint-agent__kicker">For you and your AI agent</p>
	<h2 id="flint-agent-title">Your agent uses your vault, not your passwords</h2>
	<p class="flint-agent__lede">
		You work in the app. Your coding agent works in the terminal. Both use the same vault, so
		an agent you ask to fix a server reaches it by its alias with <code>flint exec</code>.
		Flint logs in, checks the host key and hands back the output. No password lands in the
		prompt, the transcript or the agent’s context.
	</p>

	<div class="flint-agent__grid">
		<div class="flint-term" role="img" aria-label="An agent session: the user asks why nginx is down on web-01; the agent lists hosts with flint, tests the nginx config through flint exec, reads the JSON result and reports the cause">
			<div class="flint-term-bar" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>agent session</span></div>
			<pre aria-hidden="true"><span class="u">› nginx is down on web-01, can you check?</span>

<span class="b">●</span> flint --json host list
  <span class="d">[&#123;"alias":"</span><span class="a">web-01</span><span class="d">","hostname":"10.0.0.5","user":"deploy", …&#125;]</span>

<span class="b">●</span> flint --json exec <span class="a">web-01</span> -- 'nginx -t'
  <span class="d">&#123;"host":"web-01","exit_code":1,"stderr":"nginx: [emerg]</span>
  <span class="d"> unknown directive \"proxy_passs\" in …/sites-enabled/app:14", …&#125;</span>

<span class="b">●</span> A typo in <span class="ok">/etc/nginx/sites-enabled/app</span>, line 14:
  <span class="ok">proxy_passs</span> → <span class="ok">proxy_pass</span>. Want me to fix it and reload?</pre>
		</div>

		<div class="flint-agent__lists">
			<div>
				<h3>Your agent gets</h3>
				<ul class="flint-agent__yes">
					<li>Your hosts by alias, group and tag</li>
					<li>Each command’s output and exit code, as JSON if it asks</li>
					<li>Errors with stable codes it can act on: <code>locked</code>, <code>host_key</code>, <code>auth</code></li>
				</ul>
			</div>
			<div>
				<h3>It never gets</h3>
				<ul class="flint-agent__no">
					<li>Passwords, private keys or passphrases. No command prints them</li>
					<li>Your master password or the vault key</li>
					<li>A way past a changed host key: Flint refuses it before logging in</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>

	<ol class="flint-agent__steps">
		<li><strong>Unlock in Flint.</strong> The app holds the vault’s key; the CLI never reads it. Locked or closed, Flint’s window asks you, naming the command that wants in.</li>
		<li><strong>Your agent asks Flint.</strong> <code>flint exec &lt;alias&gt; -- …</code> from any agent that can run a command: Claude Code, Codex, a script.</li>
		<li><strong>Flint does the login.</strong> Jump hosts, keys, passwords and host keys stay on Flint’s side. Only the result comes back.</li>
	</ol>

	<div class="flint-agent__next">
		<p>
			<strong>Today</strong>, while the vault is unlocked in Flint, any program on your machine that can run
			<code>flint</code> can do what your agent does; it locks on its own when you step away. <strong>Next</strong>,
			Flint approves each program that asks for the vault in its own window, limited to the hosts you choose
			and for a set time, and shows you every <code>sudo</code> command before it runs.
		</p>
		<p class="flint-agent__links">
			<a href="/guides/ai-agents/">Use Flint with an AI agent <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
			<a href="/reference/security-model/">Security model <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
		</p>
	</div>
</section>
